Let the vertices of the pentagon be $A(-1,-1)$, $B(-3,4)$, $C(1,7)$, $D(6,5)$, and $E(3,-1)$.
We can use the Shoelace Theorem to find the area of the pentagon. The Shoelace Theorem states that the area of a polygon with vertices $(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), ..., (x_n, y_n)$ is given by
$$ A = \frac{1}{2} |(x_1y_2 + x_2y_3 + \cdots + x_{n-1}y_n + x_ny_1) - (y_1x_2 + y_2x_3 + \cdots + y_{n-1}x_n + y_nx_1)| $$
For our pentagon, we have:
$A(-1,-1)$, $B(-3,4)$, $C(1,7)$, $D(6,5)$, $E(3,-1)$.
\begin{align*} x_1 &= -1, \ y_1 = -1 \\ x_2 &= -3, \ y_2 = 4 \\ x_3 &= 1, \ y_3 = 7 \\ x_4 &= 6, \ y_4 = 5 \\ x_5 &= 3, \ y_5 = -1 \end{align*} 
Now we compute the two sums:
\begin{align*} \sum_1 &= x_1y_2 + x_2y_3 + x_3y_4 + x_4y_5 + x_5y_1 \\ &= (-1)(4) + (-3)(7) + (1)(5) + (6)(-1) + (3)(-1) \\ &= -4 - 21 + 5 - 6 - 3 \\ &= -29 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} \sum_2 &= y_1x_2 + y_2x_3 + y_3x_4 + y_4x_5 + y_5x_1 \\ &= (-1)(-3) + (4)(1) + (7)(6) + (5)(3) + (-1)(-1) \\ &= 3 + 4 + 42 + 15 + 1 \\ &= 65 \end{align*}
This is incorrect, let us try a different order:
Given the vertices of a convex pentagon as $(-1,-1), (-3,4), (1,7), (6,5), (3,-1)$.
The vertices are $(-1,-1), (-3,4), (1,7), (6,5), (3,-1)$.
\begin{align*} \sum_1 &= (-1)(4) + (-3)(7) + (1)(5) + (6)(-1) + (3)(-1) \\ &= -4 - 21 + 5 - 6 - 3 \\ &= -29 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} \sum_2 &= (-1)(-3) + (4)(1) + (7)(6) + (5)(3) + (-1)(-1) \\ &= 3 + 4 + 42 + 15 + 1 \\ &= 65 \end{align*} 
So the area is $\frac{1}{2} | -29 - 65| = \frac{1}{2} |-94| = \frac{1}{2} (94) = 47$.
Therefore, the area of the pentagon is 47 square units.

\boxed{47 \text{ square units}}
47

