We can use the shoelace formula to find the area of the pentagon. The vertices are $(-1, -1), (-3, 4), (1, 7), (6, 5)$ and $(3, -1)$.
We list the coordinates in counterclockwise order, and multiply the x-coordinate of each vertex by the y-coordinate of the next vertex, and sum the results. We do the same with the y-coordinate of each vertex and the x-coordinate of the next vertex, then subtract the second sum from the first.
$\begin{aligned} \text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} |(-1)(4) + (-3)(7) + (1)(5) + (6)(-1) + (3)(-1) - (-1)(-3) - (4)(1) - (7)(6) - (5)(3) - (-1)(-1)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |(-4) + (-21) + 5 + (-6) + (-3) - (3) - 4 - 42 - 15 - 1| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |(-4 - 21 + 5 - 6 - 3) - (3 + 4 + 42 + 15 + 1)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |(-29) - (65)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |-29 - 65| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |-94| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} (94) \\ &= 47 \end{aligned}$
Thus, the area of the pentagon is 47.

\boxed{47 \text{ square units}} 
\boxed{47}

