For the lines to be coplanar, the vectors $\mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1$ must be orthogonal to the cross product of $\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\mathbf{v}_2$. First, let's find $\mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1$:
\[ \mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}. \]
Next, let's find the cross product of $\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\mathbf{v}_2$:
\begin{align*} \mathbf{v}_1 \times \mathbf{v}_2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \times \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} (k)(7) - (7)(4) \\ (7)(1) - (3)(7) \\ (3)(4) - (k)(1) \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ 7 - 21 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ -14 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix} \end{align*}
Now, we want the dot product of $\mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1$ and $\mathbf{v}_1 \times \mathbf{v}_2$ to be zero:
\begin{align*} (\mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1) \cdot (\mathbf{v}_1 \times \mathbf{v}_2) &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ -14 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix} \\ &= 3(7k - 28) + 7(-14) + 11(12 - k) \\ &= 21k - 84 - 98 + 132 - 11k \\ &= 10k - 40 = 0 \end{align*}
Thus, $10k = 40$, so $k = 4$.

